### Seam walk — drive the activity-construction bridge over HTTP.
###
### Run the host:  dotnet run --project src/Apps/Elsa.Server/Elsa.Server.csproj
### Use with the REST Client (VS Code / Visual Studio / Rider).
###
### On boot, the CLR reconciliation source scans the host output folder. With the Publishing Groundwork
### bridge composed, each source-owned CLR version is committed with stable provider/consumer identity
### and its immutable executable template. The requests below add another provider-neutral CLR activity,
### list the catalog, then cross the construction seam.
###
### NOTE: CShells path-routing mounts the default shell under "/default" (see appsettings
### CShells:Shells:default). All routes below are therefore under {{host}}/default/...

@host = http://localhost:5095/default

### 1. Add a provider-neutral CLR activity catalog row.
POST {{host}}/design/activities/definitions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "activityTypeKey": "Acme.Activities.SendGreeting",
  "sourceKind": "Json",
  "sourceId": "example-catalog",
  "providerKey": "elsa.clr-activity",
  "providerSchemaVersion": "1",
  "consumerKey": "elsa.clr-activity",
  "consumerSchemaVersion": "1",
  "descriptorPayload": {
    "typeAlias": "Acme.Activities.SendGreeting"
  },
  "category": "Examples",
  "displayName": "Send greeting"
}

### 2. List constructable versions and inspect their stable provider/consumer key/schema identity.
GET {{host}}/publishing/activities

### 3. Inspect the compiled contract surface for one row. Paste an ActivityVersionId from step 2.
###    CLR rows expose canonical type aliases and stable plain-property inputs. WorkflowIdentity rows remain
###    discovery metadata until the typed child-workflow execution boundary is implemented.
GET {{host}}/publishing/activities/<paste ActivityVersionId>/construct

### Starting another workflow remains the separate ExecuteWorkflow API/activity behavior. Reusable graph
### activities use the `elsa.graph-activity` consumer and execute inside the current workflow execution.
